ry" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(2,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=y(x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)(1-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(4,-2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(4,-2)} \\\\  & =(-2)(4-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(2,2)} \\\\  & =(2)(2-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Given that the point $(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate ${y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}.$\n\n${y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}=$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & =(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =(2{y}^{2})^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =4y{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute $(2{y}^{2})^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =4y{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =4y(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} & =(8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x})\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} \\\\  & =8(-1)^{3}+4(-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,2)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,2)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(0,2)} \\\\  & =2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\pi ,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+0-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)}=1\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\pi ,1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(\\pi ,1),$ therefore we cannot have a local maximum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\pi ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of those listed"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,4),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}-(4) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+6x+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(-{x}^{3}+6x+y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =-(1)^{3}+6(1)+(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(1,4).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "234e455c2dee377dfe715b4cdab3567ed786226ac9b4cba358209b69c859b258" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/state.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff58676550e44dc400467358c6732bfddd8dcce979c9bcfdd8240e507c99ce" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =(y + x)^3.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(1,1)$\n\n- $(-4,4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7918.json" "file_sha256" "a15df1015a649e8c56516294eb934d61043b561c0e5ac73a8426f267422c7197" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that $f'(c)=0.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0+0)^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1+1)^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-4,4)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(-4,4)} \\\\  & =(4+(-4))^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7918.json" "file_sha256" "a15df1015a649e8c56516294eb934d61043b561c0e5ac73a8426f267422c7197" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4y - e^{3x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7923.json" "file_sha256" "e2bc1558e91f4a54034ad7fe7db87514a78723033b93d5717b3fc1bf498d7cdd" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y-{e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(4y-{e}^{3x})-3{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =16y-7{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(16y-7{e}^{3x})|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =16(1)-7{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7923.json" "file_sha256" "e2bc1558e91f4a54034ad7fe7db87514a78723033b93d5717b3fc1bf498d7cdd" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 - 4 - y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(2,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7924.json" "file_sha256" "95d0ec9300598e7c0b262528fc4f729ad43808a4e77e71f32d1f594de8857282" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(2,0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,0)} & ={({x}^{2}-4-y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4-(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2x-({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(2,0)} & ={(-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & =-(2)^{2}+2(2)+4+(0) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(2,0)} = 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(2,0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n\\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}  \\right|_{(2,0)}  > 0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/example-7924.json" "file_sha256" "95d0ec9300598e7c0b262528fc4f729ad43808a4e77e71f32d1f594de8857282" "basis" 1240 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14035334/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-89414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(1,-2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(-1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(-1,-2)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(1) \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,-2)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(2,-2)$\n- $(e,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,0)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(1,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,-2)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(2,-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{4} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(e,1)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(e,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the points are the stationary points of the function.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the points are the stationary points of the function"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the points are the stationary points of the function"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-163812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$\n- $(\\pi ,-1)$\n- $(0,-1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-163812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}{y}^{′}=y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(\\pi ,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,-1)} & ={(\\frac{y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{y}^{2}})|}_{(0,-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-163812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-163812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-163812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-163812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(2,2)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=y(x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)(1-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(4,-2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(4,-2)} \\\\  & =(-2)(4-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(2,2)} \\\\  & =(2)(2-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89869" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(1,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(-1,-1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(-1,-1)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1)^{2}-2(-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,0)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}-2(0) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89872" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89872" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Given that the point $(1,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate ${y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}.$\n\n${y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)}=$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & =(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =(2{y}^{2})^{′}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =4y{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute $(2{y}^{2})^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =4y{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =4y(2{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} & =(8{y}^{3}+4y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x})\\,{|}_{(1,-1)} \\\\  & =8(-1)^{3}+4(-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-352601" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3(3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =9y+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(9y+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =9(1)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89878" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89878" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-352578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $P(0,0)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{(0,0)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-352578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2(2y+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4y+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{(0,0)} & =(4y+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,{|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =4(0)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-352578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-352578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-352578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}.$ Given that the point $(1,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & =({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =({y}^{2})^{′}+({x}^{2})^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =2y{y}^{′}+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute $({y}^{2})^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =2y{y}^{′}+2x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =2y({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})+2x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =2{y}^{3}+2y{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(2{y}^{3}+2y{x}^{2}+2x)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =2(1)^{3}+2(1)(1)^{2}+2(1) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,2)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,2)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(0,2)} \\\\  & =2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89879" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{3}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(1,2)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(1,2)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,2),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(2{x}^{3}-y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =2(1)^{3}-(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =6(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3}+(2) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=0,\n$$\n\n we obtain that $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(1,2).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)}>0,\n$$\n\n we have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89428" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89428" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,-3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,-3)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,-3)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,-3),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-3)} & ={(\\frac{x}{y})|}_{(0,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{(-3)} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot y-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-x\\cdot \\frac{x}{y})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-3)} & ={(\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}})|}_{(0,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)^{2}-{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-3)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(0,-3).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-3)}<0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local maximum (not a local minimum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89895" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(1,4)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,4),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}-(4) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+6x+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(-{x}^{3}+6x+y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =-(1)^{3}+6(1)+(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(1,4).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0+4-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89888" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\pi ,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+0-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)}=1\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\pi ,1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(\\pi ,1),$ therefore we cannot have a local maximum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\pi ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a3ba25409e04db5f5e21eb39a8e2f3b5251bf45daabc994ed203836df03b6405"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "q-89887" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of those listed"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e39dad3d0831c056d905e972cedb8c60b21276123507b75572ac5ccd9d84bf7"} {"question" "e-7918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =(y + x)^3.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(1,1)$\n\n- $(-4,4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/example-7918.json" "file_sha256" "a15df1015a649e8c56516294eb934d61043b561c0e5ac73a8426f267422c7197"} {"question" "e-7918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that $f'(c)=0.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0+0)^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1+1)^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-4,4)} & ={(y+x)^{3}|}_{(-4,4)} \\\\  & =(4+(-4))^{3} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/example-7918.json" "file_sha256" "a15df1015a649e8c56516294eb934d61043b561c0e5ac73a8426f267422c7197"} {"question" "e-7923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4y - e^{3x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/example-7923.json" "file_sha256" "e2bc1558e91f4a54034ad7fe7db87514a78723033b93d5717b3fc1bf498d7cdd"} {"question" "e-7923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y-{e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(4y-{e}^{3x})-3{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =16y-7{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(16y-7{e}^{3x})|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =16(1)-7{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/example-7923.json" "file_sha256" "e2bc1558e91f4a54034ad7fe7db87514a78723033b93d5717b3fc1bf498d7cdd"} {"question" "e-7924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 - 4 - y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(2,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n\n- $(2,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039828/example-7924.json" "file_sha256" "95d0ec9300598e7c0b262528fc4f729ad43808a4e77e71f32d1f594de8857282"} {"question" "e-7924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(2,0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,0)} & ={({x}^{2}-4-y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4-(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2x-({x}^{2}-4-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(2,0)} & ={(-{x}^{2}+2x+4+y)|}_{(2,0)} \\\\  & =-(2)^{2}+2(2)+4+(0) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(2,0)} = 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(2,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(2,0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n\\left. \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}  \\right|_{(2,0)}  > 0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/1